(2305274, 314608, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Lone Kayaker" is a nice addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I think its a little harder to get a critique on a shot that you took a couple years back.  But hopefully it is still helpful to you. <br/> <br/>The shot is a very interesting overhead approach to an action shot.  I like the composition, nothing feels cropped out.  This is some excellent timing on the shot as well as capturing the water. I think it was a great job timing the water to make it look more like an action shot then the flowing water that looks more artistic and peaceful.  The colors are nice and bright in contrast to the rushing water.  I think you did a great job in this shot.  Its nice to see you return to the challenges and I look forward to your next entry.  <br/></td>', '*Critique Club* "Lone Kayaker" is a nice addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I think its a little harder to get a critique on a shot that you took a couple years back.  But hopefully it is still helpful to you.  The shot is a very interesting overhead approach to an action shot.  I like the composition, nothing feels cropped out.  This is some excellent timing on the shot as well as capturing the water. I think it was a great job timing the water to make it look more like an action shot then the flowing water that looks more artistic and peaceful.  The colors are nice and bright in contrast to the rushing water.  I think you did a great job in this shot.  Its nice to see you return to the challenges and I look forward to your next entry.  ', '2006-04-12 01:50:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305302, 314581, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"Touch" is a wonderful addition into the Best of 2002 challenge. I have to say wow and congrats on the top 20% especially in a challenge that was based on past work. <br/> <br/>This is just an amazing shot. It captures so much and I could easily see it on an inspiration poster. For whatever reason you decide to take this shoot back in 2002 it was defiantly worth it. I really like your use of light, by creating the shadow off to the left. I really love the positioning you choose, I think you made an great choice with leaving an odd number of hands in the shot. What is interesting is that normally I would say that the cropping on the hands on the bottom seems a little off, but it is such a great piece I really did not notice. There is not much I can say because it is just a great piece.<br/> <br/>Congrats on the score as well!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - fixed a CC glitch.</i></td>', '*Critique Club*  "Touch" is a wonderful addition into the Best of 2002 challenge. I have to say wow and congrats on the top 20% especially in a challenge that was based on past work.  This is just an amazing shot. It captures so much and I could easily see it on an inspiration poster. For whatever reason you decide to take this shoot back in 2002 it was defiantly worth it. I really like your use of light, by creating the shadow off to the left. I really love the positioning you choose, I think you made an great choice with leaving an odd number of hands in the shot. What is interesting is that normally I would say that the cropping on the hands on the bottom seems a little off, but it is such a great piece I really did not notice. There is not much I can say because it is just a great piece. Congrats on the score as well!Message edited by HBunch - fixed a CC glitch.', '2006-04-12 01:58:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305392, 314550, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Alaska" is a wonderful addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I had to peak at your profile before I started the critique to see if you lived in Alaska.  I have to say WOW you have been to some amazing places and have the shots to prove it.  <br/> <br/>This is an amazing shot that I am sure many of us will never see something like this in our lifetimes.  I think I like this shot because of how massive the mountains are as well as how deep they are.  The composition is nice, the include of the sky make the image feel cold to me.  The picture does look unreal but just amazing.  I really like how smooth the first mountain and how well it flows in to the rest of the them.  I personally wouldn''t change anything in the shot.  Congrats and thanks for sharing!</td>', '*Critique Club* "Alaska" is a wonderful addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I had to peak at your profile before I started the critique to see if you lived in Alaska.  I have to say WOW you have been to some amazing places and have the shots to prove it.   This is an amazing shot that I am sure many of us will never see something like this in our lifetimes.  I think I like this shot because of how massive the mountains are as well as how deep they are.  The composition is nice, the include of the sky make the image feel cold to me.  The picture does look unreal but just amazing.  I really like how smooth the first mountain and how well it flows in to the rest of the them.  I personally wouldn''t change anything in the shot.  Congrats and thanks for sharing!', '2006-04-12 02:19:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305445, 314493, 48560, '<td valign="top">Wow, how could I have missed this during voting?<br/>Lovely, lovely! And this from a point-n-shoot camera :)<br/>Good job, very interesting colours and tonal. Congrats on the ribbon!</td>', 'Wow, how could I have missed this during voting?Lovely, lovely! And this from a point-n-shoot camera :)Good job, very interesting colours and tonal. Congrats on the ribbon!', '2006-04-12 02:34:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305464, 314494, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Window to the Soul" was personally one of my favorites into the Best of 2002 challenge.  Something about this piece just grabbed my attention, I am really surprised it didn''t to better.  But I guess everyone has very different opinions about what is appealing. <br/> <br/>The reflection is awesome, I think you had a wonderful concept.  I like how the reflection is what is staring at us the viewer.  The composition is great and has a nice background in contrast to the orange suit.  Maybe voters just had a hard time with the piece because it was a toy and we had the Playtime and Children''s Toy just about at the same time.  Technically I think this is a great piece and personally I really like your work.  I look forward to your future entries.</td>', '*Critique Club* "Window to the Soul" was personally one of my favorites into the Best of 2002 challenge.  Something about this piece just grabbed my attention, I am really surprised it didn''t to better.  But I guess everyone has very different opinions about what is appealing.  The reflection is awesome, I think you had a wonderful concept.  I like how the reflection is what is staring at us the viewer.  The composition is great and has a nice background in contrast to the orange suit.  Maybe voters just had a hard time with the piece because it was a toy and we had the Playtime and Children''s Toy just about at the same time.  Technically I think this is a great piece and personally I really like your work.  I look forward to your future entries.', '2006-04-12 02:41:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305491, 314761, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"Capitol Spring" is a nice photograph to have in the Best of 2002 challenge. It is interesting in your comments how you stated that this is one of your first digital pictures because it looks very nice. <br/> <br/>I really like the perspective you took and created a foreground out of the trees. You were able to take an object that is often shot in many boring pictures and were able to approach it in a different way. The composition is lovely and feels nice and full. Very interesting choice on the frame, I really think frames are a hard thing to pull off. I would be curious to see the shot with a thinner solid black frame. The grey frame is a little too distracting for me. <br/> <br/>Thanks for entering Capitol Spring, it was really nice it see a different perspective. I hope you continue to enter challenges.<br/> <br/>Also I like the shadows that are cast from the trees.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - fixed a CC glitch.</i></td>', '*Critique Club*  "Capitol Spring" is a nice photograph to have in the Best of 2002 challenge. It is interesting in your comments how you stated that this is one of your first digital pictures because it looks very nice.  I really like the perspective you took and created a foreground out of the trees. You were able to take an object that is often shot in many boring pictures and were able to approach it in a different way. The composition is lovely and feels nice and full. Very interesting choice on the frame, I really think frames are a hard thing to pull off. I would be curious to see the shot with a thinner solid black frame. The grey frame is a little too distracting for me.  Thanks for entering Capitol Spring, it was really nice it see a different perspective. I hope you continue to enter challenges. Also I like the shadows that are cast from the trees.Message edited by HBunch - fixed a CC glitch.', '2006-04-12 02:48:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305532, 314480, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Rays of Light" is an amazing entry into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I am amazed looking at the differences between the original and entry, you did a great job.  It seems so wrong to receive a 7+ and not ribbon, like others have said.  .<br/> <br/>This is an excellent piece, it is cropped just perfectly.  The angle is very nice is creating the atmosphere.  The rays of light are so fitting for the church environment.  There is not much I can comment on because you did such excellent job.  Congrats on the score and of course I look forward to all of your future entries.</td>', '*Critique Club* "Rays of Light" is an amazing entry into the Best of 2002 challenge.  I am amazed looking at the differences between the original and entry, you did a great job.  It seems so wrong to receive a 7+ and not ribbon, like others have said.  . This is an excellent piece, it is cropped just perfectly.  The angle is very nice is creating the atmosphere.  The rays of light are so fitting for the church environment.  There is not much I can comment on because you did such excellent job.  Congrats on the score and of course I look forward to all of your future entries.', '2006-04-12 02:59:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305638, 314910, 48524, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Hi, Paul.  Comparing with your original shot, the color correction was fairly well-done and in good taste.  I especially like how you brought out the blues in the sky and the water; it''s very vivid.  I also enjoy the composition a lot...I don''t often see a tall and narrow crop on a coastline shot like this, and it emphasizes the uneven, jagged edges very well.  I especially like how the distant horizon fades into clouds and mist.<br/> <br/>One thing I noticed first, though, is the greens of the treeline down the middle of the picture.  Especially in the shadows, the colors feel kind of hazy and flat, and it makes your color-correction seem very evident at that specific point.  Since there were no editting restrictions, I think just a tiny bit of burning on the shadows would have brought out a little more definition, and maybe also on the grass in the foreground as well.<br/> <br/>I think it was a good choice for you not to burn the shoreline; the way it seems to just fade into the clouds at the point where it meets the water is quite nice, and gives it sort of an airy, almost dreamy feel.  Though it may just be the way the fog builds on the water (it''s less evident in your original shot since there isn''t as much contrast in that area), the horizon seems a little bit crooked, and I think you could have rotated the image a little bit clockwise to straighten it out.<br/> <br/>The title is somewhat on the bland side, in my opinion, but it''s direct and doesn''t really detract anyway.<br/> <br/>Nice shot overall...makes me miss the ocean.  It''s been a while since I''ve seen one :o</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Hi, Paul.  Comparing with your original shot, the color correction was fairly well-done and in good taste.  I especially like how you brought out the blues in the sky and the water; it''s very vivid.  I also enjoy the composition a lot...I don''t often see a tall and narrow crop on a coastline shot like this, and it emphasizes the uneven, jagged edges very well.  I especially like how the distant horizon fades into clouds and mist. One thing I noticed first, though, is the greens of the treeline down the middle of the picture.  Especially in the shadows, the colors feel kind of hazy and flat, and it makes your color-correction seem very evident at that specific point.  Since there were no editting restrictions, I think just a tiny bit of burning on the shadows would have brought out a little more definition, and maybe also on the grass in the foreground as well. I think it was a good choice for you not to burn the shoreline; the way it seems to just fade into the clouds at the point where it meets the water is quite nice, and gives it sort of an airy, almost dreamy feel.  Though it may just be the way the fog builds on the water (it''s less evident in your original shot since there isn''t as much contrast in that area), the horizon seems a little bit crooked, and I think you could have rotated the image a little bit clockwise to straighten it out. The title is somewhat on the bland side, in my opinion, but it''s direct and doesn''t really detract anyway. Nice shot overall...makes me miss the ocean.  It''s been a while since I''ve seen one :o', '2006-04-12 03:59:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305671, 314412, 48524, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Hi, Wágner.  What a creepy shot!  This definitely jumped out at me when I first saw it just now (i didn''t vote or look through any of the 02 submissions last week), so it''s totally got the wow-factor.  The extreme tilt is something I''m rather a fan of and use myself fairly often, and it''s very appropriate in this shot.  I have absolutely no complaints about the composition (and I usually do find something to complain about), it''s just that everything seems to work so well.  Separately, it seems very off-balance, but since the whole shot is so slanted it all comes together in the end.<br/> <br/>Despite such strong diagonals, which usually imply movement, there''s a sense of heavy immobility, and even though the ground is at such an angle everything feels static and grounded.  <br/> <br/>The tones are perfect, the stark white of the pillars set off by the dark grey of the face and the black of the shadows under the roof, the little details in the carving standing out just right...<br/> <br/>I really have nothing much else to say about it.  It''s just a very amazing and powerful shot to me, it takes a lot of the elements of photography that I love and puts it all together.  Awesome shot :)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Hi, Wágner.  What a creepy shot!  This definitely jumped out at me when I first saw it just now (i didn''t vote or look through any of the 02 submissions last week), so it''s totally got the wow-factor.  The extreme tilt is something I''m rather a fan of and use myself fairly often, and it''s very appropriate in this shot.  I have absolutely no complaints about the composition (and I usually do find something to complain about), it''s just that everything seems to work so well.  Separately, it seems very off-balance, but since the whole shot is so slanted it all comes together in the end. Despite such strong diagonals, which usually imply movement, there''s a sense of heavy immobility, and even though the ground is at such an angle everything feels static and grounded.   The tones are perfect, the stark white of the pillars set off by the dark grey of the face and the black of the shadows under the roof, the little details in the carving standing out just right... I really have nothing much else to say about it.  It''s just a very amazing and powerful shot to me, it takes a lot of the elements of photography that I love and puts it all together.  Awesome shot :)', '2006-04-12 04:21:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(2305684, 314896, 48524, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Hi, Larry.  First of all, welcome with your first challenge...it looks like you''ve been here a while but didn''t decide to submit something until now.  First of all, making use of the full 640px dimension limit will definitely increase your score.  Also, one of the first things that struck me with your picture is how overblown the whites are...I guess you used the on-board flash for it, from the harsh shadows of the petals as well.<br/> <br/>As far as composition goes, the image is fairly well-balanced, but it also lacks interest in my opinion.  There are flowers there, and some leaves and buds, but other than that I can''t really pick out something I feel drawn to focus on.  The top half, where all the leaves come together, feels a little messy and kind of distracting.  I think if you had focused and cropped closer to show just the bottom few blooms it would have worked a little better (although I kind of do like the added detail of the buds on the side)...it''s all your call, I suppose.<br/> <br/>Anyway, keep submitting to challenges!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Hi, Larry.  First of all, welcome with your first challenge...it looks like you''ve been here a while but didn''t decide to submit something until now.  First of all, making use of the full 640px dimension limit will definitely increase your score.  Also, one of the first things that struck me with your picture is how overblown the whites are...I guess you used the on-board flash for it, from the harsh shadows of the petals as well. As far as composition goes, the image is fairly well-balanced, but it also lacks interest in my opinion.  There are flowers there, and some leaves and buds, but other than that I can''t really pick out something I feel drawn to focus on.  The top half, where all the leaves come together, feels a little messy and kind of distracting.  I think if you had focused and cropped closer to show just the bottom few blooms it would have worked a little better (although I kind of do like the added detail of the buds on the side)...it''s all your call, I suppose. Anyway, keep submitting to challenges!', '2006-04-12 04:31:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(2307072, 314431, 32767, '<td valign="top">Ooooh boy....Death by over processing.</td>', 'Ooooh boy....Death by over processing.', '2006-04-12 13:55:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308002, 314480, 55881, '<td valign="top">I would be glad to know how you did the rays of light! Do update me if you did the tutorial. :)</td>', 'I would be glad to know how you did the rays of light! Do update me if you did the tutorial. :)', '2006-04-12 18:58:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308626, 314682, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club.<br/> <br/>What I really like about this image is the sense of balance and symmetry that''s here. It''s busy, but at the same time all the elements work together. I like the strong lines, and then angle that this was shot from. I especially like the shadow on the wall, it just brings everything together.<br/> <br/>I think though, that this might benefit from being a little more sharp &amp; crisp, it would give it extra "pop". I''d also like to see the colours more vivid, particularly the red. <br/> <br/>All in all, this is a very unique and memorable image. Good job. :)</td>', 'Hello from the critique club. What I really like about this image is the sense of balance and symmetry that''s here. It''s busy, but at the same time all the elements work together. I like the strong lines, and then angle that this was shot from. I especially like the shadow on the wall, it just brings everything together. I think though, that this might benefit from being a little more sharp & crisp, it would give it extra "pop". I''d also like to see the colours more vivid, particularly the red.  All in all, this is a very unique and memorable image. Good job. :)', '2006-04-12 21:59:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308644, 314673, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club!<br/> <br/>Overall, I think that this is a pretty good image. I like the branch framing the shot and how it goes down into the image, so as to not just appear stuck there in the way, it becomes part of the composition.<br/> <br/>This images seems a little dark to me though, enough so that small and interesting details are lost. I think some lightening would give this more depth and visual interest. I''d like to see this in colour, since all the similar tones can get a little smallowed up with this in B &amp; W.<br/> <br/>The clouds turned out quite lovely; they add softness to the harder edges around them.<br/> <br/>This is a good image, I just think that a little extra editing would make it great. :)<br/> <br/>-Monica</td>', 'Hello from the critique club! Overall, I think that this is a pretty good image. I like the branch framing the shot and how it goes down into the image, so as to not just appear stuck there in the way, it becomes part of the composition. This images seems a little dark to me though, enough so that small and interesting details are lost. I think some lightening would give this more depth and visual interest. I''d like to see this in colour, since all the similar tones can get a little smallowed up with this in B & W. The clouds turned out quite lovely; they add softness to the harder edges around them. This is a good image, I just think that a little extra editing would make it great. :) -Monica', '2006-04-12 22:06:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308662, 314707, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club!<br/> <br/>First of all, this is a really good image. I like how the man-made buildings are reflecting the beauty of nature, it''s almost ironic really, that nature is shining through the objects that have been built up to block it out. <br/> <br/>The golden colours are really nice, they complement the blues of the sky fantastically. The building in between is well framed by the others reflecting the sky, without that in there this would be a boring shot. <br/> <br/>The only thing I can think of that might improve this would be the angle of the outer bulidings, particuarly the one to my right. The angle seems just a bit wonky, I would like it straight to complement the rigid and geometrically perfect shapes of the buildings. That would probably be terribly hard to do though, but a small crop off the top could help.<br/> <br/>All in all this is a lovely image, you should be proud of it. :)<br/> <br/>-Monica</td>', 'Hello from the critique club! First of all, this is a really good image. I like how the man-made buildings are reflecting the beauty of nature, it''s almost ironic really, that nature is shining through the objects that have been built up to block it out.  The golden colours are really nice, they complement the blues of the sky fantastically. The building in between is well framed by the others reflecting the sky, without that in there this would be a boring shot.  The only thing I can think of that might improve this would be the angle of the outer bulidings, particuarly the one to my right. The angle seems just a bit wonky, I would like it straight to complement the rigid and geometrically perfect shapes of the buildings. That would probably be terribly hard to do though, but a small crop off the top could help. All in all this is a lovely image, you should be proud of it. :) -Monica', '2006-04-12 22:13:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308681, 314446, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>As soon as I saw this image I thought of Neopolitan ice cream, the colours just say that to me.<br/> <br/>This shot is technically sufficient, but it just doesn''t seem that interesting to me. I like the choice of a dark background, but to really say Automn, I think the colours should be slightly warmer, they seem just a bit too neutral to me. The glow is nice, but some of the holder is a bit blown out in spots on the rim, it''s distracting. Also on the background in the right hand corner the surface is showing whereas it''s not in any other place, so that too is somewhat distracting.<br/> <br/>All in all, this is a good image, and I''m glad it''s selling well for you. :)<br/> <br/>-Monica</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! As soon as I saw this image I thought of Neopolitan ice cream, the colours just say that to me. This shot is technically sufficient, but it just doesn''t seem that interesting to me. I like the choice of a dark background, but to really say Automn, I think the colours should be slightly warmer, they seem just a bit too neutral to me. The glow is nice, but some of the holder is a bit blown out in spots on the rim, it''s distracting. Also on the background in the right hand corner the surface is showing whereas it''s not in any other place, so that too is somewhat distracting. All in all, this is a good image, and I''m glad it''s selling well for you. :) -Monica', '2006-04-12 22:18:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308786, 314707, 36284, '<td valign="top">great shot!</td>', 'great shot!', '2006-04-12 22:57:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2308999, 314663, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Romeo" is a funny addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.   I love fun shots, that is what I was really expecting from the 2002 challenge.  <br/> <br/>This piece itself is nice.  I wish your husband was in focus but heck it is just a great shot.  The lighting is interesting how it is just capturing him from the right hand side of the image.  I like the border it makes the photograph feel more complete, also a nice addition since the windows are framed.  Overall I think this was a fun photo and I am glad you entered it.   I can obviously tell from your portfolio that your technical skills have greatly improve and your creativity is still present!</td>', '*Critique Club* "Romeo" is a funny addition into the Best of 2002 challenge.   I love fun shots, that is what I was really expecting from the 2002 challenge.   This piece itself is nice.  I wish your husband was in focus but heck it is just a great shot.  The lighting is interesting how it is just capturing him from the right hand side of the image.  I like the border it makes the photograph feel more complete, also a nice addition since the windows are framed.  Overall I think this was a fun photo and I am glad you entered it.   I can obviously tell from your portfolio that your technical skills have greatly improve and your creativity is still present!', '2006-04-13 00:57:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309016, 315031, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"JOY is Merry on Christmas!" is a nice candid in the Best of 2002 challenge.  <br/> <br/>Obviously you were capturing a true expression, which is always wonderful to see.  The image itself is a little harsh.  The image looks over processed because there are areas that are very pixeled, especially in her hair.  There are also some weird white lines that come across in the shot, possibly from the lights reflection. Overall the shot seems out of focus, but I can understand that you were trying to capture the emotion.</td>', '*Critique Club* "JOY is Merry on Christmas!" is a nice candid in the Best of 2002 challenge.   Obviously you were capturing a true expression, which is always wonderful to see.  The image itself is a little harsh.  The image looks over processed because there are areas that are very pixeled, especially in her hair.  There are also some weird white lines that come across in the shot, possibly from the lights reflection. Overall the shot seems out of focus, but I can understand that you were trying to capture the emotion.', '2006-04-13 01:08:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309019, 314448, 47152, '<td valign="top">Oh my gosh that''s amazing!</td>', 'Oh my gosh that''s amazing!', '2006-04-13 01:10:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309058, 314448, 5112, '<td valign="top">Congratulations!  What a beautiful sunset.  Some things never change.</td>', 'Congratulations!  What a beautiful sunset.  Some things never change.', '2006-04-13 01:33:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309224, 314493, 34938, '<td valign="top">exquisite in every way...congratulations on your first ribbon. Well done!</td>', 'exquisite in every way...congratulations on your first ribbon. Well done!', '2006-04-13 02:30:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309433, 314448, 56859, '<td valign="top">Amazing composition and color saturation.....Congrats!!!</td>', 'Amazing composition and color saturation.....Congrats!!!', '2006-04-13 06:55:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309788, 314543, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Well first of all, it''s nice to see something risky than the usual ''safe'' entries people usually enter. Taking the chance of a low score with something a little different is good, and you recieved a reasonable score this time, so congrats.<br/> <br/>This image is neat, just not everyone''s taste. I don''t really know what to say that could help since you''ve most likely intended for it to turn out this way.<br/> <br/>I would''ve liked perhaps that the two main people were in better focus while keeping the lively movements of the background characters, but that is a choice entirely up to you. I like how the image gives off a sense of movement and energy.<br/> <br/>All in all this is quite an interesting shot and if you''re happy with it than you''ve done your job. Keep experimenting!</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! Well first of all, it''s nice to see something risky than the usual ''safe'' entries people usually enter. Taking the chance of a low score with something a little different is good, and you recieved a reasonable score this time, so congrats. This image is neat, just not everyone''s taste. I don''t really know what to say that could help since you''ve most likely intended for it to turn out this way. I would''ve liked perhaps that the two main people were in better focus while keeping the lively movements of the background characters, but that is a choice entirely up to you. I like how the image gives off a sense of movement and energy. All in all this is quite an interesting shot and if you''re happy with it than you''ve done your job. Keep experimenting!', '2006-04-13 11:11:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309799, 314508, 52336, '<td valign="top">There''s a lot of great activity in this beautifully composed photograph.  The items in the air are the obvious focal point of activity, their blurred forms creating highly interesting wedge shapes against the almost perfectly black background.  The juggler''s shirt, with its wavy horizontal lines, add a kind of counter-motion to the vertical thrust of the image, and the creases in the shiny pants support the effect wonderfully.  Add to that the fact that, with this image, frozen at this moment, it appears almost as though the juggler is willing the items to stay aloft with an upward gesture of his arms, and you''ve got a very satisfying tension between all the elements here.  The crop is perfect, adding just the right amount of solid ground for him to stand on, while still leave us with a feeling of dizzying motion.<br/> <br/>Duotone seems to be an obvious choice here, and the correct one, in my opinion, supporting the well-executed theme of motion by not detracting with too much colour, which may have added a "busy" feel to the image.<br/> <br/>If it had been possible to capture the juggler in sharper focus, or to do so in post, my feeling is the effect would have been outstanding.  As it is, a great image, and very satisfying.  Thanks!<br/> <br/>Hope this helps,<br/>Louis</td>', 'There''s a lot of great activity in this beautifully composed photograph.  The items in the air are the obvious focal point of activity, their blurred forms creating highly interesting wedge shapes against the almost perfectly black background.  The juggler''s shirt, with its wavy horizontal lines, add a kind of counter-motion to the vertical thrust of the image, and the creases in the shiny pants support the effect wonderfully.  Add to that the fact that, with this image, frozen at this moment, it appears almost as though the juggler is willing the items to stay aloft with an upward gesture of his arms, and you''ve got a very satisfying tension between all the elements here.  The crop is perfect, adding just the right amount of solid ground for him to stand on, while still leave us with a feeling of dizzying motion. Duotone seems to be an obvious choice here, and the correct one, in my opinion, supporting the well-executed theme of motion by not detracting with too much colour, which may have added a "busy" feel to the image. If it had been possible to capture the juggler in sharper focus, or to do so in post, my feeling is the effect would have been outstanding.  As it is, a great image, and very satisfying.  Thanks! Hope this helps,Louis', '2006-04-13 11:16:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2309801, 314729, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First of all, this is an interesting, candid shot. It does however, have a snapshotish quality to it that seems a little bland to me.<br/> <br/>This image would benefit from being more sharp, as well as having the colours more vivid. The details of the feathers are lost on the bird, he would be much more interesting if you could see all the little feathers on him. The cage is very distracting, but may have not been an option to remove it. If that''s the case it''s very unfortunate because it would be a much better image without it. <br/> <br/>The photo has a certain charm to it and is amusing. It generates a response from the viewer, so it is a successful shot.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! First of all, this is an interesting, candid shot. It does however, have a snapshotish quality to it that seems a little bland to me. This image would benefit from being more sharp, as well as having the colours more vivid. The details of the feathers are lost on the bird, he would be much more interesting if you could see all the little feathers on him. The cage is very distracting, but may have not been an option to remove it. If that''s the case it''s very unfortunate because it would be a much better image without it.  The photo has a certain charm to it and is amusing. It generates a response from the viewer, so it is a successful shot.', '2006-04-13 11:17:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2310215, 314578, 53316, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/><i>First, let me say that I am not a professional or even a very good amature photographer, so you may want to take my comments with a grain of salt. </i> <br/> <br/><b>Meeting the Challenge:</b> Well, I''d certainly say this qualifies. :-)<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> Wow. I''ve never seen a photo that so wonderfully balances foreground and background.  The diagonal line of the land works very well too.  I am very impressed by the composition and this is what makes the photo really stand out for me.<br/> <br/>I''m assuming this is an accident (if not, wow), but the silhouette has a couple of pieces of the foreground cutting across and it looks very much like the collar of a jacket or something.  It''s really cool.<br/> <br/><b>Color:</b> Three words: to die for.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work / Post Processing / Lighting:</b> Everything''s in focus that should be and with a picture like this... it really makes it amazing.  I love the crop, and the contrast and saturation look great to me. As mentioned previously, a burn on some of the highlights in the front might have helped. (This is the ONLY thing I see about this photo that I don''t love 100%)  I love the way the light from the background is balanced with the shadows.<br/> <br/><b>Title:</b> I don''t completely understand but am thinking it has a relation to the evening, and that totally works with this photo. In any case I think it is a good title because it doesn''t state something obvious about the picture (letting the picture speak for itself).<br/> <br/><b>Image Dimensions and Filesize:</b> No problems here.<br/> <br/><b>Misc / My subjective thoughts:</b> Wow.  Just wow. I am so delighted I got to critique this photo. Have you considered making it available as a print? (I''d recommend the burning and maybe a border color closer to the tan of the foreground if you do.)<br/> <br/><i>I hope this helps! Feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</i> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First, let me say that I am not a professional or even a very good amature photographer, so you may want to take my comments with a grain of salt.   Meeting the Challenge: Well, I''d certainly say this qualifies. :-) Composition: Wow. I''ve never seen a photo that so wonderfully balances foreground and background.  The diagonal line of the land works very well too.  I am very impressed by the composition and this is what makes the photo really stand out for me. I''m assuming this is an accident (if not, wow), but the silhouette has a couple of pieces of the foreground cutting across and it looks very much like the collar of a jacket or something.  It''s really cool. Color: Three words: to die for. Camera Work / Post Processing / Lighting: Everything''s in focus that should be and with a picture like this... it really makes it amazing.  I love the crop, and the contrast and saturation look great to me. As mentioned previously, a burn on some of the highlights in the front might have helped. (This is the ONLY thing I see about this photo that I don''t love 100%)  I love the way the light from the background is balanced with the shadows. Title: I don''t completely understand but am thinking it has a relation to the evening, and that totally works with this photo. In any case I think it is a good title because it doesn''t state something obvious about the picture (letting the picture speak for itself). Image Dimensions and Filesize: No problems here. Misc / My subjective thoughts: Wow.  Just wow. I am so delighted I got to critique this photo. Have you considered making it available as a print? (I''d recommend the burning and maybe a border color closer to the tan of the foreground if you do.) I hope this helps! Feel free to PM me if you have any questions. ', '2006-04-13 14:42:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(2310274, 314971, 53316, '<td valign="top">Only my sixth critique, and I get your image? Er, of <b>course</b> I''m not intimidated...!  ;-)<br/> <br/><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/><i>First, let me say that I am not a professional or even a very good amature photographer, so you may want to take my comments with a grain of salt. </i> <br/> <br/>I love the silhouettes (although as mentioned before, the leaning aspect is there) and think that they are quite interesting looking. There is a lot of detail there, which is very nice.  The crop works very well.  However I do think that what could have made this a vastly improved image is the sky. I would have loved to see more of the brighter blue and less of the grey blue, for a greater contrast.  As it is, your picture is a very good one technically but it doesn''t seem to have the impact that many of your other photos have.<br/> <br/><i>I hope this helps! Feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</i></td>', 'Only my sixth critique, and I get your image? Er, of course I''m not intimidated...!  ;-) Greetings from the Critique Club!  First, let me say that I am not a professional or even a very good amature photographer, so you may want to take my comments with a grain of salt.   I love the silhouettes (although as mentioned before, the leaning aspect is there) and think that they are quite interesting looking. There is a lot of detail there, which is very nice.  The crop works very well.  However I do think that what could have made this a vastly improved image is the sky. I would have loved to see more of the brighter blue and less of the grey blue, for a greater contrast.  As it is, your picture is a very good one technically but it doesn''t seem to have the impact that many of your other photos have. I hope this helps! Feel free to PM me if you have any questions.', '2006-04-13 15:13:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2311035, 314448, 25678, '<td valign="top">Go Doc!!<br/> <br/>OK, I propose you stop traveling so much and send me somewhere intstead! :D<br/> <br/>Congratulations!!</td>', 'Go Doc!! OK, I propose you stop traveling so much and send me somewhere intstead! :D Congratulations!!', '2006-04-13 21:56:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(2311107, 314758, 27901, '<td valign="top">Wow, this one is simply fantastic! congratulations on your ribbon!</td>', 'Wow, this one is simply fantastic! congratulations on your ribbon!', '2006-04-13 22:27:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2311643, 314828, 19831, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-) <br/> <br/>Well, this shot obviously fits the challenge :-) I must admit I have been a bit perplexed since I pulled this shot from the queue for a critique a couple of days ago. Just puzzled over why a pretty decent photog with nice equipment, some great shots in their port (a 4th place among them) and a 5.75+ avg would want a formal critique on a shot taken with a p&amp;s at a party 4 yrs ago, but anyways...<br/> <br/>The first thing that strikes me is the wild expression. Kind of engaging at first, but starts to seem out of place with the nice, solid black backgroung. Makes sense when you read your notes, it fits for a party shot, that is what it comes across as. I wonder if maybe this might have scored better if there were a little detail in the background to give some context? Maybe not. Skin tone looks a tad bit on the yellow side. There are some harsh shadows, probably due to onboard flash? It''s a bit disconcerting to me how her black top totally blends into the background, it makes her neck look kind of un-natural and almost cut n pasted like. Same with how we lose part of the earrings. <br/> <br/>So overall, the expression has some interest, and captures your attention initially, but then the overall quality I feel allows the viewer to quickly be ready to ''move on'', and leave wondering if this was perhaps a doctored up ''party shot''. <br/> <br/>If you have any questions or comments or anything, please feel free to contact me. <br/> <br/>Happy shooting, <br/>taterbug :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-)  Well, this shot obviously fits the challenge :-) I must admit I have been a bit perplexed since I pulled this shot from the queue for a critique a couple of days ago. Just puzzled over why a pretty decent photog with nice equipment, some great shots in their port (a 4th place among them) and a 5.75+ avg would want a formal critique on a shot taken with a p&s at a party 4 yrs ago, but anyways... The first thing that strikes me is the wild expression. Kind of engaging at first, but starts to seem out of place with the nice, solid black backgroung. Makes sense when you read your notes, it fits for a party shot, that is what it comes across as. I wonder if maybe this might have scored better if there were a little detail in the background to give some context? Maybe not. Skin tone looks a tad bit on the yellow side. There are some harsh shadows, probably due to onboard flash? It''s a bit disconcerting to me how her black top totally blends into the background, it makes her neck look kind of un-natural and almost cut n pasted like. Same with how we lose part of the earrings.  So overall, the expression has some interest, and captures your attention initially, but then the overall quality I feel allows the viewer to quickly be ready to ''move on'', and leave wondering if this was perhaps a doctored up ''party shot''.  If you have any questions or comments or anything, please feel free to contact me.  Happy shooting, taterbug :-)', '2006-04-14 03:11:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(2311812, 314578, 35720, '<td valign="top">Thanks a lot Sandy for your kind comment.<br/> <br/>And many thanks to klstover for his good critique.</td>', 'Thanks a lot Sandy for your kind comment. And many thanks to klstover for his good critique.', '2006-04-14 06:38:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2312769, 314933, 52336, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>Overall, a nice image with an immediate impact, particularly for those of us who live in a part of the world where an picture like this brings back great memories of childhood.  The colours are satisfying for a photograph that''s mostly snow.  Composition is done nicely, the three-quarter view of the house a good choice.<br/> <br/>The snow gives the photo a noisy quality that detracts for me, although it is indeed immediately recognizable as snow.  It also has the effect of giving the entire composition a somewhat soft feel, which does not benefit the photograph.  The large dark tree at left draws the eye persistently; perhaps a tighter crop around the house would have avoided this.  Similarly, the house almost "floats" above a very soft undefined area of snow in the foreground, which is just slightly too heavy for my taste.  Overall tone and exposure leaves the image slightly flat.  Perhaps some work in post could have popped the house and made the elements around it recede somewhat.<br/> <br/>Overall, though, a good photo, and one that really does bring back wintry memories of my childhood.  Thanks. :)<br/> <br/>Hope this helps,<br/>Louis<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-04-14 16:04:22.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Overall, a nice image with an immediate impact, particularly for those of us who live in a part of the world where an picture like this brings back great memories of childhood.  The colours are satisfying for a photograph that''s mostly snow.  Composition is done nicely, the three-quarter view of the house a good choice. The snow gives the photo a noisy quality that detracts for me, although it is indeed immediately recognizable as snow.  It also has the effect of giving the entire composition a somewhat soft feel, which does not benefit the photograph.  The large dark tree at left draws the eye persistently; perhaps a tighter crop around the house would have avoided this.  Similarly, the house almost "floats" above a very soft undefined area of snow in the foreground, which is just slightly too heavy for my taste.  Overall tone and exposure leaves the image slightly flat.  Perhaps some work in post could have popped the house and made the elements around it recede somewhat. Overall, though, a good photo, and one that really does bring back wintry memories of my childhood.  Thanks. :) Hope this helps,Louis', '2006-04-14 17:03:08.000000', '2006-04-14 17:04:22.000000', 0),
(2313033, 314493, 5728, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your ribbon, super image!</td>', 'Congrats on your ribbon, super image!', '2006-04-14 20:02:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(2313049, 314572, 28359, '<td valign="top">You were robbed, should had scored higher. Great composition and angles, the forground blur works.....should had finished in top 100 imo.....ace</td>', 'You were robbed, should had scored higher. Great composition and angles, the forground blur works.....should had finished in top 100 imo.....ace', '2006-04-14 20:17:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(2314309, 314795, 52731, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Well I really like this photo. It''s kind of trippy, and I am immediately drawn into the subject.<br/> <br/>I would like to see some more details on the people though, just have them nice and crisp with the rest remained blurred towards them.<br/> <br/>This kind of experiment is great, I love when people try out techniques different from most of the others.<br/> <br/>Good job.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! Well I really like this photo. It''s kind of trippy, and I am immediately drawn into the subject. I would like to see some more details on the people though, just have them nice and crisp with the rest remained blurred towards them. This kind of experiment is great, I love when people try out techniques different from most of the others. Good job.', '2006-04-15 14:37:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2315220, 314915, 25884, '<td valign="top">Hola from the critique club.. :)<br/> <br/>ok, im gonna start off by telling you this is a good image for something taken in 2002. When one enters a challenge such as best of 2002 one must consider the caliber of person he is going up against. Many of the people on dpc are professional''s, this considered many of them probably had dSLR''s in 2002. <br/> <br/>Now to the image. Not to be artsy and critical but composition lacks, mainly because you didnt follow the rule of thirds, i know this is a snapshot but that would have boosted your score i little. I also think that you could have eddited this a little bit more in order to make the subject ''pop'' more. i say this because in order for the photograph to be succesfull it must possess a main subject, in this photograph the lighting was flat, causing everything to look the same.<br/> <br/>I understand that this is just a snapshot, and that you weren''t really taking into acount the rule of thirds and the lighting and such, but in order to score well here, you have to do that, even if it is just a snapshot. <br/> <br/>if you have any comments or questions please feel free to PM me. :)<br/> <br/>hope i helped,<br/> <br/>-Dan</td>', 'Hola from the critique club.. :) ok, im gonna start off by telling you this is a good image for something taken in 2002. When one enters a challenge such as best of 2002 one must consider the caliber of person he is going up against. Many of the people on dpc are professional''s, this considered many of them probably had dSLR''s in 2002.  Now to the image. Not to be artsy and critical but composition lacks, mainly because you didnt follow the rule of thirds, i know this is a snapshot but that would have boosted your score i little. I also think that you could have eddited this a little bit more in order to make the subject ''pop'' more. i say this because in order for the photograph to be succesfull it must possess a main subject, in this photograph the lighting was flat, causing everything to look the same. I understand that this is just a snapshot, and that you weren''t really taking into acount the rule of thirds and the lighting and such, but in order to score well here, you have to do that, even if it is just a snapshot.  if you have any comments or questions please feel free to PM me. :) hope i helped, -Dan', '2006-04-16 00:11:08.000000', NULL, 0),
